WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.960
There's more to this drone than meets the eye.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:13.140
The team behind this 'bot took inspiration from&nbsp;
nature to design a device capable of morphing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:19.380
to suit its environment, transforming to most&nbsp;
efficiently tackle the challenge in front of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:35.040
Animals use their bodies in various ways to suit&nbsp;
different terrain. Meerkats may not be great at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:40.020
walking on two legs but they can stand up when&nbsp;
they need to to scout out their surroundings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.860
Turtle flippers may be optimized for swimming&nbsp;
but they can also haul the reptiles up a beach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:52.440
when needed. And it was this ability to&nbsp;
use appendages for multiple functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.440 --> 00:01:00.420
that inspired the design of this robot named the&nbsp;
multimodal mobility morphobot or M4 for short.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:08.040
M4 isn't the first robot to move in multiple ways&nbsp;
but the researchers behind it say that previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:15.180
designs mostly fell into one of two camps those&nbsp;
with 'morpho' functionality like this robot that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:22.500
can reshape its limbs to walk or swim, or those&nbsp;
that use redundancy - having a set of wheels and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.720
propeller blades to travel on land or the sea. But&nbsp;
M4 is different, it can perform a variety of tasks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:37.860
by using its same limbs in different ways just&nbsp;
like an animal changing its posture or gait - thus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:44.640
finding a sweet spot between redundancy and the&nbsp;
ability to change shape and function. In practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:51.720
what this means is that M4 can fly and roll as&nbsp;
you might expect, but also tumble over obstacles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:01.200
Balance on one end. Crouch under&nbsp;
barriers. And crawl over rocky terrain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.080
It can even manipulate this ball using&nbsp;
two of its propellers like hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:15.360
When presented with a 45 degree ramp too steep&nbsp;
for wheels alone, the team took inspiration from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.840
the Chukka Partridge which uses its wings to&nbsp;
help it run up inclines. They instructed M4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:29.340
to do something similar. M4 could of course just&nbsp;
fly up the ramp, but rotors use much more energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:36.180
than wheels and so by combining the two modes M4&nbsp;
can achieve the same goal much more efficiently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.460
The researchers argued that this morphobot&nbsp;
could be used for everything from search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.200
and rescue to future space missions and&nbsp;
want to expand on their design to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.900
M4 more sophisticated tools and greater&nbsp;
articulation. They are also working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:57.900
algorithms that could allow M4 to interpret&nbsp;
data from sensors and autonomously choose its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:04.440
mode of transport based on its surroundings -&nbsp;
be that rolling flying or something in between.

